185	  Let \(P\) be a prime divisor whose point lies in a nonempty affine open
186	  \(U\), with Dedekind chart \(A_U\)
187	  (\Cref{thm:adelic_chartRing_isDedekindDomain}). The prime \(P\)
188	  determines a height-one prime \(\mathfrak p_P\) of \(A_U\), and the
189	  discrete valuation of the challenge,
190	  \(v_P = \mathrm{order}\, P\) on \(K\) (\Cref{def:order_at_point}),
191	  coincides with the \(\mathfrak p_P\)-adic valuation of \(A_U\) on
192	  \(K = \operatorname{Frac} A_U\). Its residue field is
193	  \(\kappa(P) = A_U/\mathfrak p_P = \struct{C, P}/\mathfrak m_P\), and the
194	  \emph{degree} of \(P\) is the finite number
195	  \(\deg P = \dim_k \kappa(P) = [\kappa(P) : k]\).
196	\end{definition}
197	\begin{proof}
198	  The stalk \(\struct{C, P}\) is the localisation \((A_U)_{\mathfrak p_P}\)
199	  because \(P\) lies in the affine \(U\); its normalised valuation, whose
200	  \(\mathrm{WithZero.log}\) is \(\mathrm{order}\, P\)
201	  (\Cref{def:order_at_point}), is by definition the
202	  \(\mathfrak p_P\)-adic valuation. Independence of the chosen chart is
203	  the naturality \Cref{lem:order_eq_order_restrict}: \(\mathrm{order}\)
204	  is invariant under open-immersion restriction, so any two affine charts
205	  containing \(P\) give the same valuation on \(K\). Finiteness of
206	  \(\deg P\): \(\kappa(P)\) is the residue field of a closed point of the
207	  finite-type \(k\)-scheme \(C\); choosing a nonconstant \(x \in K\)
208	  bounds \([\kappa(P) : k] \le [K : k(x)] < \infty\).
209	\end{proof}
